Subject: add /run and vendor tiers to the compiled XML and SGML catalog
  defaults
Source: downstream patch for libxml2 2.13.5
Upstream: not submitted; needs agreement on the /usr/share/xml,
  /usr/share/sgml, /run/xml, and /run/sgml paths

catalog.c:55-59 built XML_XML_DEFAULT_CATALOG from
"file://" SYSCONFDIR "/xml/catalog" and XML_SGML_DEFAULT_CATALOG the same
way from SYSCONFDIR "/sgml/catalog". Makefile.am:15 passes
-DSYSCONFDIR='"$(sysconfdir)"', and the package manifest ran ./configure
--prefix=/usr with no --sysconfdir, so autotools kept its ${prefix}/etc
default and both constants compiled to /usr/etc: a path under the
immutable /usr tree that a machine owner can never write. There was also
no vendor or /run tier, so a package could not ship a default alias and a
transient override had nowhere to go. This was confirmed against a built
artifact: the Nex-built libxml2.so.2.13.5 inside the desktop rootfs
snapshot carries exactly the string file:///usr/etc/xml/catalog and no
/etc/xml/catalog at all.

xmlInitializeCatalog() (catalog.c:3070) reads XML_CATALOG_FILES when set,
or XML_XML_DEFAULT_CATALOG otherwise, and walks that string as a
space-separated list, creating one XML_CATA_CATALOG entry per element and
chaining them through nextent in the order they appear. Making the
compiled default a space-separated list of three file:// URIs therefore
needs no new parsing code: the existing space-separated-list walk already
handles it, whichever source supplied the value.

xmlCatalogListXMLResolve() (catalog.c:1970) walks that same chain forward
and returns on the first entry that resolves the requested identifier:

    while (catal != NULL) {
        if (catal->type == XML_CATA_CATALOG) {
            ...
            ret = xmlCatalogXMLResolve(catal->children, pubID, sysID);
            if (ret != NULL) {
                break;
            ...
        catal = catal->next;
    }

so the first matching entry in the chain wins, not the last. This patch
therefore orders each list with the administrator's SYSCONFDIR entry
first, then /run, then the vendor entry last, so an administrator mapping
beats the same mapping read from /run, which beats the vendor default.
This is the opposite chain order from the popt repair in this tree, where
handleAlias() walks backward and the last entry read wins; the two
patches read their own reader's resolution order rather than assuming a
shared shape.

A missing base file in any tier stays silent: xmlFetchXMLCatalogFile()
(catalog.c:1376) calls xmlParseXMLCatalogFile(), which calls
xmlParseCatalogFile(), which calls xmlParserInputBufferCreateFilename();
a nonexistent path makes that call return NULL without printing anything
(the "Failed to parse catalog" trace only fires when XML_DEBUG_CATALOG is
set), xmlFetchXMLCatalogFile() then marks the entry XML_CATA_BROKEN_CATALOG
and returns -1, and xmlCatalogListXMLResolve() finds catal->children still
NULL and moves on to catal->next. Adding two more entries to the chain
does not change this: each absent tier is skipped the same way the single
compiled tier already was skipped.

Neither new tier installs anything under /etc or /usr/etc:
/usr/share/xml, /usr/share/sgml, /run/xml, and /run/sgml are only
compiled-in search paths that this package does not populate.

XML_SGML_DEFAULT_CATALOG is not read anywhere else in this version of
catalog.c; xmlInitializeCatalog() only auto-loads the XML default. The
constant is still patched identically for consistency with the XML
default and because it keeps the same #ifndef override hook available
to a future caller.
--- a/catalog.c
+++ b/catalog.c
@@ -56 +56,4 @@
-#define XML_XML_DEFAULT_CATALOG "file://" SYSCONFDIR "/xml/catalog"
+#define XML_XML_DEFAULT_CATALOG \
+    "file://" SYSCONFDIR "/xml/catalog" \
+    " file:///run/xml/catalog" \
+    " file:///usr/share/xml/catalog"
@@ -59 +62,4 @@
-#define XML_SGML_DEFAULT_CATALOG "file://" SYSCONFDIR "/sgml/catalog"
+#define XML_SGML_DEFAULT_CATALOG \
+    "file://" SYSCONFDIR "/sgml/catalog" \
+    " file:///run/sgml/catalog" \
+    " file:///usr/share/sgml/catalog"
